Not yet. With the swearing-in of the cabinet only minutes away, a loose end begins to unravel.

Mallin, being unaware of Goodryke's promise to [+ royalist_leader +] that elections would immediately be called, was taken aback by [+ royalist_chancellor +] requesting a commitment on that point, and firmly refused to accept [+ royalist_leader +]'s demand.

With Mallin uncompromising as ever, no matter what is conceded to him, his arrogance has actually paid off this time. Not until Marsden interrupts, and Mallin, seeing himself as the only obstacle, accedes to new elections.

In an act of perjury, the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Royalist</span> leader swore to defend and uphold the constitution and laws of the republic he had so long vowed to destroy.

With DuFour believing that he was appointing a parliamentary chancellor, he ordered negotiations with the <span style="color: #000000;">Unionist Party</span>, which were purposefully sabotaged and ultimately failed.

What do we do?

- @nazi_5

@nazi_5
title: Review our options.
on-display: {!  
    var cardEl = document.createElement('div');  
    cardEl.className = "face-figure2";  

