Unfortunately, not many <span style="color: #000000;">repu</span><span style="color: #DD0000;">bli</span><span style="color: #FFCC00;">cans</span> are willing to take up arms against the newly appointed [+ royalist_chancellor +].

Do we realize that this means civil war, against the full force of the state?

- @civil_war: Yes.
- @bide_time: We are not ready for war. We must bide our time.

@nazi_6
title: Mallin saves democracy?
subtitle: His arrogance has finally become useful.
unavailable-subtitle: He is satisfied with his ministries. 
choose-if: hugenberg_gallax_r <= -2

Not yet. With the swearing-in of the cabinet only minutes away, a loose end begins to unravel.

Mallin, unaware of Goodryke's promise to [+ royalist_leader +] that elections would be immediately called, is taken aback when [+ royalist_chancellor +] requests a commitment on this point and firmly refuses to accept [+ royalist_leader +]'s demand. His relatively strong performance in the last election has only inflated his ego.

With Mallin as uncompromising as ever, no matter what is conceded to him, his arrogance has actually paid off this time.

Even when Marsden interrupts, Mallin—the only obstacle to [+ royalist_chancellor +]'s ascension—storms off after [+ royalist_leader +] continues to stubbornly reject his demand. With the leader of the only relevant party willing to coalition with them having left, DuFour's illusions that the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span> would form a parliamentary cabinet are shattered.

- @nazi_7

@nazi_7
title: Who will succeed Staal now?
on-arrival: chancellor = "Goodryke"; heartland_leader = "Goodryke"; papen_back = 1

As Goodryke continues pleading with DuFour to reconsider appointing [+ royalist_chancellor +], he has effectively removed himself from consideration. Nevertheless, DuFour still regards it as an urgent priority to remove Staal—the chancellor rumored to be preparing a coup against him—from office.

Goodryke is once again reluctantly persuaded to form a presidential cabinet, with himself at its head and most of Staal's ministers retained. However, this is only a temporary solution: a purely caretaker government, DuFour stresses, while a new <i>parliamentary</i> cabinet is assembled. A vote of no confidence looms.